   "source": [
    "#5. Interpretation:\n",
    "#a. Interpret the coefficients of the logistic regression model.\n",
    "#b. Discuss the significance of features in predicting the target variable (survival probability in this case).\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 34,
   "id": "9f67ad78-43ca-42d0-952e-2b087156a9c2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#a. Interpret the coefficients of the logistic regression model.\n",
    "#Logistic regression coefficients explain how each feature affects the probability of diabetes.\n",
    "#- Positive coefficient: Increases the likelihood of diabetes.\n",
    "#- Negative coefficient: Decreases the likelihood of diabetes.\n",
    "#- Coefficient close to zero: Has a smaller influence on the prediction.\n",
    "#Since the features were standardized, the coefficients represent the effect of a one-standard-deviation increase in each feature.\n",
    "#b. Discuss the significance of features in predicting the target variable.\n",
    "#The model uses eight features: Pregnancies, Glucose, Blood Pressure, Skin Thickness, Insulin, BMI, Diabetes Pedigree Function, and Age.\n",
    "\n",
    "#These features provide health-related information that helps predict diabetes. Their relative influence can be examined using the absolu#te coefficient values. Features with larger absolute coefficients have a stronger influence on the model's predictions.\n",
    "\n",
    "#Conclusion: Logistic regression uses these features to estimate the probability of diabetes. Each feature contributes differently to the final prediction."
   ]
  },
